# Source: Slides "ICT3112 - 7 - Recepy for Loop Invariants - exercises", Exercise 3 and Exercise 5 (b).
id: sum-of-odd-numbers
title: Sum of the first n odd numbers
topic: Loop invariants
level: 2
source: Slides 7, Recipe exercises, Exercises 3 and 5 b
tags: recipe, invariant, failed candidate

task:
  Find the invariant by generalising the postcondition, give a variant,
  and check establishment and preservation. A trace with a few rows helps.

pre: n >= 0
post: s = n^2

program:
  {P}
  {R4}
  i := 0;                  // i1
  {R3}
  s := 0;                  // i2
  {I}
  while (i < n) do {
    {I && i < n}
    {R2}
    s := s + (2 * i + 1);  // b1
    {R1}
    i := i + 1             // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(i < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: A candidate that keeps n
rule: LOGIC
text:
  Exercise 5 (b) of the slides proposes the candidate $s = n^2 && 0 <= i <= n$,
  which only adds the bounds to the postcondition. Establishment fails:
vc: {n >= 0} i := 0; s := 0 {s = n^2 && 0 <= i <= n}
status: fails
counterexample: n = 2, i = 0, s = 0
why:
  Right after the initialisation $i = 0$ and $s = 0$. With $n = 2$ the
  candidate asks for $s = 4$. The candidate rejects a real state, so it is
  too strong. The fix is to replace $n$ by $i$.

step: Trace and generalise
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  | At the loop head | $i$ | $s$ |
  |---|---|---|
  | before iteration 1 | $0$ | $0$ |
  | before iteration 2 | $1$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 3 | $2$ | $4$ |
  | before iteration 4 | $3$ | $9$ |
set: I = s = i^2 && 0 <= i <= n

step: Establishment
rule: ASSIGN, SEQ, WP
focus: i1, i2
calc:
  wp(s := 0, I)
  = 0 = i^2 && 0 <= i <= n
set: R3 = 0 = i^2 && 0 <= i <= n
calc:
  wp(i := 0, R3)
  = 0 = 0^2 && 0 <= 0 <= n
  = 0 <= n
set: R4 = 0 <= n
vc: n >= 0 ==> 0 <= n

step: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, SEQ, CONSEQUENCE
focus: b1, b2
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, I)
  = s = (i + 1)^2 && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
set: R1 = s = (i + 1)^2 && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
calc:
  wp(s := s + (2 * i + 1), R1)
  = s + (2 * i + 1) = (i + 1)^2 && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
set: R2 = s + (2 * i + 1) = (i + 1)^2 && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
vc: s = i^2 && 0 <= i <= n && i < n ==> s + (2 * i + 1) = (i + 1)^2 && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
why:
  $(i + 1)^2 = i^2 + 2 * i + 1$, and $s = i^2$.

step: Exit and termination
rule: CONSEQUENCE, WHILE-TOTAL
vc: s = i^2 && 0 <= i <= n && !(i < n) ==> s = n^2
why:
  At the exit $i = n$, so $s = n^2$.
vc: {s = i^2 && 0 <= i <= n && i < n && n - i = E0} s := s + (2 * i + 1); i := i + 1 {n - i < E0}
why:
  The variant $n - i$ decreases by one in every iteration, and it is
  positive when an iteration starts.
result: The triple is valid.
